        "id": "ecb7686c-fb4b-4e61-9af7-091835468904",
        "url": "https://www.facebook.com/SpaceFP/posts/on-september-24-2025-nasa-successfully-launched-the-interstellar-mapping-and-acc/832868435927881",
        "canonical_url": "https://www.facebook.com/SpaceFP/posts/on-september-24-2025-nasa-successfully-launched-the-interstellar-mapping-and-acc/832868435927881",
        "title": "24K views · 439 reactions | On September 24, 2025, NASA successfully launched the Interstellar Mapping and Acceleration Probe (IMAP), along with two secondary spacecraft, aboard a SpaceX Falcon 9 rocket. The mission's primary objective is to map the boundaries of the heliosphere, the giant protective bubble created by the solar wind that shields our solar system from harmful cosmic radiation.\n\nLaunch and deployment details\n\nLaunch vehicle: A SpaceX Falcon 9 rocket launched from Launch Complex 39A at NASA's Kennedy Space Center in Florida.\n\nLiftoff: The mission lifted off on Wednesday, September 24, 2025, at 7:30 a.m. EDT.\n\nRideshare payloads: In addition to IMAP, the rocket also carried NOAA's Space Weather Follow On-Lagrange 1 (SWFO-L1) and NASA's Carruthers Geocorona Observatory.\n\nOrbital destination: All three spacecraft are traveling to the Sun-Earth L1 Lagrange Point, a gravitationally stable position approximately 1 million miles from Earth. They are expected to arrive in January 2026.\n\nSignal acquisition: IMAP successfully established communications with mission control shortly after its deployment from the rocket's second stage.\n\nBooster landing: The Falcon 9 first-stage booster successfully landed on the droneship \"Just Read the Instructions\" in the Atlantic Ocean.\n\nDennis Hlavsa /@dhlavsa with Space FrontPage and Launch Window Photo \n\n#NASA #SpaceX #falcon9 #IMAP #launchvideo #NOAA | Space FrontPage",
